lude W1:4, W2:3, W3:2, W4:3, W5:2, W6:3, W7:2, W8:4, W9:0. Ts have T2:4, T3:1, T4:3, T5:2, T6:4, T7:2, T8:2, T9:1. Bs have B1:3, B2:3, B3:4, B4:2, B5:2, B6:0, B7:0, B8:2, B9:3. \n\nLooking at my hand, maybe if I play a tile that allows me to form a pung or chow. For example, if I play B8, maybe someone else can't form a pung with it. Alternatively, if I play W1, maybe someone else can't form a pung. But I need to think about my own hand.\n\nWait, maybe I can form a chow in Bs. I have B1, B3, B4. If I can get a B2, I can form B1-2-3. But B2 is available (unshown count is 3). So if I can get a B2, that would help. But since I'm the one to play, maybe playing a tile that doesn't block my own possibilities.\n\nLooking at my valid actions: Play B8, W2, W7, W1, B1, T7, B4, T2, W4, T3, B3.\n\nIf I play B8, that's a terminal tile. Since I have a W9 pung, maybe I need to avoid giving up tiles that others might need. But the player across has T7-8-9, so maybe T8 is important for them. If I play T7, that could help them complete a chow if they have T8. But I have T7 in my hand. Wait, I have T7 in my hand. If I play T7, the player across might be able to form a chow with T7-8-9. But they already have T7-8-9 as a meld? Wait, the melds of the player across are [[T7, T8, T9]], so they already have that chow. So playing T7 might not help them further. But maybe another player can use it. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, if I play B4, which I have one of. The unshown B4 count is 2. If someone else has two B4s, they might be forming a pung. But since I have one, playing it might not block them. But I need to consider my own hand. If I play B4, maybe I can later form a chow with B3-4-5 if I get a B5. But B5 is available (unshown count 2). But I don't have B5 in my hand. So maybe that's a possibility.\n\nAlternatively, if I play W2, which I have one of. The unshown W2 count is 3. If I play W2, maybe someone else can form a pung. But I don't have two W2s, so they can't form a pung from my play. W