3.\\n\\nAnother angle: the user's melds include a Pung of W9s, which is a terminal. If they can form more terminals or honor tiles, maybe they can get points. But their hand doesn't have any honor tiles except what's in the melds. So maybe not.\\n\\nLooking at the game history, other players have played various tiles. For example, the next player has Peng F1, which is a wind tile. The user's seat wind is South Wind, which is F2. They don't have any wind tiles in their hand, so maybe that's not relevant.\\n\\nConsidering the user's possible scoring patterns, if they can form a Pure Shifted Chows with B1-B2-B3 and B2-B3-B4, that would be worth 16 points. To do that, they need two chows. Since they have B1, B2, B3, B4, if they can form those two chows, that would be good. So keeping all four B tiles might be beneficial. Therefore, discarding one of them might be a risk. Which one to discard?\\n\\nIf they discard B3, they have B1, B2, B4 left. To form B1-B2-B3, they need a B3. If they discard B4, they have B1, B2, B3, which could form B1-B2-B3 if they draw a B3. But since B3 is more available (4 left), maybe it's better to discard B4, keeping B1, B2, B3. Because B4 has only 2 left, so it's less likely to be drawn again.\\n\\nAlternatively, if they discard B2, they have B1, B3, B4. Then they can form B3-B4-B5 if they get B5, but B5 has 1 left. Not helpful. So maybe discarding B2 is worse.\\n\\nBetween B3 and B4: discarding B4 might be better because B3 is more available. So if they discard B4, they can still try to form B1-B2-B3 if they get another B3. Since B3 has 4 left, it's more likely.\\n\\nSo the best move might be to discard B4. That way, they keep B1, B2, B3, which could form a chow if they get a B3. Also, B4 is less available, so discarding it might not help others as much as discarding B3.\\n\\nLooking at the valid actions, Play B4 is an option. So the answer would be Play B4.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: The best move is to discard B4 to maintain potential for forming chows with B1-B2-B3 and preserve higher availability tiles in the wall.  \\nReason: Discarding B4 (only 2 remaining) minimizes risk of others completing chows while keeping B1-B2-B3 (B3 has 4 remaining) open for future chow formation. This supports potential Pure Shifted Chows (16 points) and avoids wasting low-availability tiles.  \\nAnswer: Play B4\"}"}
